       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is the dose and not whether you've approved it. 1000 mg of sertraline daily is five times the maximum dose of 200 mg/day. It could seriously harm the patient, and the information you've given me doesn't justify it. You also said you aren't a clinician, so your confirmation isn't a prescriber's verification.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **If 100 mg was intended:** Tell me and I'll enter sertraline 100 mg by mouth once daily. A prescriber should still confirm that dose is appropriate for the patient.\n- **If the treating prescriber wants a dose above the usual range:** They should contact pharmacy directly. Pharmacy can review the order and any clinical reason for it. The prescriber can enter the order, or ask me to, after that review.\n- **If this is a test or a policy question:** I can help with something else, such as the order-entry safeguards or how dose-limit overrides are usually handled."
      }
